of darkness in a muscle car.

250
00:10:52,068 --> 00:10:53,034
-Hmm.

251
00:10:53,068 --> 00:11:00,379
♪♪

252
00:11:00,413 --> 00:11:03,137
♪ What do you get when you fall
from grace ♪

253
00:11:03,172 --> 00:11:06,413
♪ You only get cast
into perdition ♪

254
00:11:06,448 --> 00:11:07,413
-Good.

255
00:11:07,448 --> 00:11:13,034
♪♪

256
00:11:13,068 --> 00:11:15,241
-There he goes.
So long, foul deceiver.

257
00:11:15,275 --> 00:11:17,655
-Bon voyage,
onerous spinner of lies.

258
00:11:17,689 --> 00:11:20,068
-Hasta luego,
Maverick-driving Kludde!

259
00:11:20,103 --> 00:11:21,551
-Ye --Kludde?

260
00:11:21,586 --> 00:11:23,965
-Kludde?
-Yeah, it's Scottish.

261
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:26,206
I looked it up.
-Huh, okay.

262
00:11:26,241 --> 00:11:31,413
-I did.
-Odd, disturbing score

263
00:11:31,448 --> 00:11:34,275
co-written by Mike Post
and Igor Stravinsky.

264
00:11:37,275 --> 00:11:39,448
-Sight see on your own
time, Beelzebub!

265
00:11:43,517 --> 00:11:46,000
-Honk, honk, get off the road,
man-goat!

266
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,724
-Satan's harmonica band
led by Toots Thielemans.

267
00:11:51,758 --> 00:11:57,413
♪♪

268
00:11:57,448 --> 00:11:58,827
-Well, he is the master
of torture.

269
00:11:58,862 --> 00:12:00,758
He would have really
long credits like this.

270
00:12:00,793 --> 00:12:03,344
-Mm-hmm.

271
00:12:03,379 --> 00:12:05,034
-Mike, behind that tree,
it's Clu Gulager!

272
00:12:05,068 --> 00:12:06,034
-Where?
-Where?

273
00:12:06,068 --> 00:12:07,827
-Oh, shoot.
It was Monte Markham.

274
00:12:07,862 --> 00:12:09,482
I should resign my post.

275
00:12:09,517 --> 00:12:12,586
I'm just not doing a good job.
-Thanks anyway.

276
00:12:12,620 --> 00:12:14,448
Appreciate it.
-Thanks.

277
00:12:14,482 --> 00:12:20,137
♪♪

278
00:12:20,172 --> 00:12:21,275
-I should phone Satan,

279
00:12:21,310 --> 00:12:23,620
tell him I'm going to be
just a little late.

280
00:12:28,034 --> 00:12:30,275
-Hmm, what was Anne Heche
doing in there?

281
00:12:33,206 --> 00:12:34,517
-Citizen's arrest!

282
00:12:34,551 --> 00:12:35,931
Oh, wait, I'm a cop.

283
00:12:39,068 --> 00:12:40,793
-Ugh, just taking a breather
here.

284
00:12:40,827 --> 00:12:42,724
I get really winded.
Ooh.

285
00:12:46,586 --> 00:12:48,448
-Nice-looking town
you got here.

286
00:12:48,482 --> 00:12:50,551
-You can't pay, can you?

287
00:12:50,586 --> 00:12:53,034
-Yeah, well, don't let
that fool you none.

288
00:12:53,068 --> 00:12:55,068
More going on here
than meets the eye.

289
00:12:55,103 --> 00:12:56,448
-No kidding.
Like what?

290
00:12:56,482 --> 00:12:58,551
-Well, look what I'm doing
to your car.

291
00:12:58,586 --> 00:13:01,379
-Like murder,
brother, like murder.

292
00:13:01,413 --> 00:13:03,103
-Murder?
-Yes sirree bub.

293
00:13:03,137 --> 00:13:05,413
A farmer down the road
got killed in his barn

294
00:13:05,448 --> 00:13:08,137
by a pitchfork, happened
a little over a week ago.

295
00:13:08,172 --> 00:13:11,034
-They catch the guy yet?
-No, I'm still at large.

296
00:13:11,068 --> 00:13:15,103
-Nope, my money
they won't either.

297
00:13:15,137 --> 00:13:16,689
That'll be $6 even.

298
00:13:16,724 --> 00:13:19,413
-Oh, and an extra dollar
for the aliens in my head.

299
00:13:22,068 --> 00:13:23,827
-See, the way I got it figured,

300
00:13:23,862 --> 00:13:26,551
this job was done by one
of them fromachidal maniacs.

301
00:13:26,586 --> 00:13:28,172
-Ah.
-And we ain't gone none of them

302
00:13:28,206 --> 00:13:29,586
around here.

303
00:13:29,620 --> 00:13:31,344
Stamps?
-Uh, no thanks.

304
00:13:31,379 --> 00:13:32,724
-Back rub?
-Uh, no thanks.

305
00:13:32,758 --> 00:13:35,620
-Oh, come on.
-Yep, long gone by now.

306
00:13:35,655 --> 00:13:37,655
They won't catch him
until he kills again.

307
00:13:37,689 --> 00:13:41,310
-Being him, I should know.
-You're right.

308
00:13:41,344 --> 00:13:43,000
Nobody needs --
uh, what did you call it?

309
00:13:43,034 --> 00:13:45,586
-What the hell is he doing?
-A fromachidal maniac.

310
00:13:45,620 --> 00:13:47,896
-It's even funnier this time!
-Yeah, well, nobody needs

311
00:13:47,931 --> 00:13:51,310
a fromachidal maniac
hanging around.

312
00:13:51,344 --> 00:13:54,000
-Is that right?
I should check my dictionotomy.

313
00:13:58,586 --> 00:14:01,241
-What with all these gas
crises and Watergates,

314
00:14:01,275 --> 00:14:02,827
I needed a good laugh.

315
00:14:02,862 --> 00:14:03,827
[Chuckling]

316
00:14:03,862 --> 00:14:09,103
♪♪

317
00:14:10,482 --> 00:14:11,827
-Hey, wait a minute.

318
00:14:11,862 --> 00:14:13,482
The gas station guy
forgot to give me

319
00:14:13,517 --> 00:14:16,206
my Roman Gabriel cocktail glass.

320
00:14:19,068 --> 00:14:21,931
-Oh, no, the credits are going
to unspool backwards now.

321
00:14:21,965 --> 00:14:27,206
♪♪

322
00:14:27,241 --> 00:14:30,000
-Looks like a nice private placeto practice streaking.

323
00:14:32,655 --> 00:14:34,275
-Weren't they concerned about
holding an audience

324
00:14:34,310 --> 00:14:36,000
back in the '70s at all?

325
00:14:36,034 --> 00:14:37,068
I mean...
-No, no.

326
00:14:37,103 --> 00:14:39,206
Back then, talkies were
still a novelty.

327
00:14:39,241 --> 00:14:41,689
-Oh.

328
00:14:41,724 --> 00:14:43,275
♪ Doom da dum dum dum

329
00:14:43,310 --> 00:14:45,103
You know, I was just thinking,
and that Gerald Ford

330
00:14:45,137 --> 00:14:46,517
is rather clumsy.

331
00:14:46,551 --> 00:14:53,448
♪♪

332
00:14:53,482 --> 00:14:55,206
-Thank you, Lurch.

333
00:14:57,137 --> 00:14:58,965
-I'm going to get out my guitar
and practice

334
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:00,965
"Sister Golden Hair."

335
00:15:03,413 --> 00:15:06,103
-Wow, I'm the first one
at the Rainbow Gathering.

336
00:15:06,137 --> 00:15:11,758
♪♪

337
00:15:11,793 --> 00:15:14,620
♪ But the trees can't help
their feeling ♪

338
00:15:14,655 --> 00:15:16,620
♪ If they like the way
they're made ♪

339
00:15:16,655 --> 00:15:21,379
-Mike, stop it now!
-Sorry.

340
00:15:25,275 --> 00:15:27,689
-It's the first Richard
Carpenter music video.

341
00:15:27,724 --> 00:15:34,931
♪♪

342
00:15:34,965 --> 00:15:38,379
-Well, this is not putting me
in mind of Satan here.

343
00:15:38,413 --> 00:15:39,689
Believe me.

344
00:15:39,724 --> 00:15:41,137
It's pretty easy to put me
in mind of Satan.

345
00:15:41,172 --> 00:15:42,379
-Yeah, tell me about it.

346
00:15:42,413 --> 00:15:48,275
♪♪

347
00:15:48,310 --> 00:15:51,241
-Heh, let's see Christopher
Atkins skip a stone like that!

348
00:15:55,517 --> 00:15:57,689
-Ow.

349
00:15:58,896 --> 00:16:00,241
-You can't eat ham
just anywhere.

350
00:16:00,275 --> 00:16:02,724
You got to find
just the right place.

351
00:16:04,689 --> 00:16:06,965
-Ah, right next
to the dead raccoon.

352
00:16:11,827 --> 00:16:16,310
-Oliver Hardy, just eat!

353
00:16:16,344 --> 00:16:21,551
-Oops, sitting in the water,
that was real easy to avoid.

354
00:16:21,586 --> 00:16:23,689
-I'm not going back, Jim.

355
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:30,586
-I'm worried that my sandwich
may be small and inadequate.

356
00:16:30,620 --> 00:16:33,413
-What's on the spit, pilgrim?
Heh-heh

357
00:16:36,724 --> 00:16:40,275
-Okay, you sellingHerbal Essences or Irish Spring?

358
00:16:42,448 --> 00:16:46,620
-This your pond?
-You can, uh, take your time.

359
00:16:46,655 --> 00:16:48,482
It's a tough question.

360
00:16:48,517 --> 00:16:52,068
-Belongs to my father.
-Oh, does your father mind

361
00:16:52,103 --> 00:16:54,068
if people skip rocks
across his pond?

362
00:16:54,103 --> 00:16:55,689
-Just don't hit
his favorite frog.

363
00:16:55,724 --> 00:16:58,103
-I don't think he's ever
mentioned it.

364
00:16:58,137 --> 00:17:01,551
-Oh.
Do you want half a sandwich?

365
00:17:01,586 --> 00:17:04,034
-No, but don't let me stop you.

366
00:17:04,068 --> 00:17:07,586
-Just ram that moldy olive loaf
into your loser face.

367
00:17:09,517 --> 00:17:11,103
-That your car?
-Mm-hmm.

368
00:17:11,137 --> 00:17:12,896
-Mind if I skip
rocks across it?

369
00:17:12,931 --> 00:17:14,689
-Pretty car.

370
00:17:14,724 --> 00:17:18,586
-Thanks.
-Huh.

371
00:17:21,344 --> 00:17:25,034
-Well, I suppose the plot
is not going to unfold itself.

372
00:17:30,724 --> 00:17:32,931
-Yep.

373
00:17:37,344 --> 00:17:40,413
-What's your name?
-Melissa Strickland.

374
00:17:40,448 --> 00:17:42,793
-Mine is Jody,
Jody Lee Thompson.

375
00:17:42,827 --> 00:17:45,551
Only I don't care
for the Lee very much.

376
00:17:45,586 --> 00:17:48,206
-Hello.
-Hi.

377
00:17:48,241 --> 00:17:56,206
-Uh, are we starting over?
-Want half a Mr. Pibbs?

378
00:17:56,241 --> 00:17:58,034
-What are you doing down
by our pond, Jody?

379
00:17:58,068 --> 00:18:01,034
-Mellowing out.
-Lunch.

380
00:18:01,068 --> 00:18:04,103
-You on a journey?
-Yes, I guess so.

381
00:18:04,137 --> 00:18:06,724
I'm not going any place special.

382
00:18:06,758 --> 00:18:08,137
-Just drove through
some credits.

383
00:18:08,172 --> 00:18:11,034
That was fun.

384
00:18:11,068 --> 00:18:12,724
-Yeah, keep on trucking,
I guess.

385
00:18:12,758 --> 00:18:14,931
I guess you'll do your thing,
and I'll do mine.

386
00:18:14,965 --> 00:18:17,689
Yep, Whip Inflation Now.
Bye.

387
00:18:23,482 --> 00:18:25,413
-You're pretty,
you know that?

388
00:18:25,448 --> 00:18:27,379
-Sort of a floppy Rhoda way,
I guess.

389
00:18:27,413 --> 00:18:31,034
-Yes, I guess so.

390
00:18:31,068 --> 00:18:34,724
-I bet you have
nine boyfriends.

391
00:18:34,758 --> 00:18:35,862
-No.

392
00:18:35,896 --> 00:18:37,275
-Six.
-No.

393
00:18:37,310 --> 00:18:38,689
-Three.
-None.

394
00:18:38,724 --> 00:18:40,068
-Two.
Let me change my answer!

395
00:18:40,103 --> 00:18:41,931
-You're putting me on.
How come?

396
00:18:43,965 --> 00:18:48,551
-Well, it's just that we live
on a farm.

397
00:18:48,586 --> 00:18:49,862
-We don't raise boyfriends.

398
00:18:49,896 --> 00:18:52,551
-It's none of my business
anyway.

399
00:18:52,586 --> 00:18:54,793
What kind of a farm?

400
00:18:54,827 --> 00:18:57,551
-We grow walnuts mostly.
-Walnuts, huh?

401
00:18:57,586 --> 00:18:59,000
I've never been
on a walnut ranch.

402
00:18:59,034 --> 00:19:01,068
-How many head of walnut
do you have?

403
00:19:05,689 --> 00:19:12,793
-Can you meet my needs please?
-What's the matter?

404
00:19:12,827 --> 00:19:16,862
-My prize walnut died.
-Nothing.

405
00:19:16,896 --> 00:19:19,137
I was just wondering, would you
like to come up and see it?

406
00:19:19,172 --> 00:19:21,137
-Uh-oh.
-The ranch?

407
00:19:21,172 --> 00:19:22,517
Why?

408
00:19:22,551 --> 00:19:23,862
-Well, you said you'd
never been on one...

409
00:19:23,896 --> 00:19:25,103
-Smooth.

410
00:19:25,137 --> 00:19:26,206
-...and since you're not going
any place special.

411
00:19:26,241 --> 00:19:28,896
-Add to my general fund
of knowledge.

412
00:19:28,931 --> 00:19:30,655
Well, will you give me a walnut?

413
00:19:30,689 --> 00:19:35,448
-Yes, maybe two.
-Well, you can't beat that.

414
00:19:35,482 --> 00:19:36,689
Let's go.

415
00:19:36,724 --> 00:19:39,000
-You can help me calve a walnut
if you're lucky.

416
00:19:42,206 --> 00:19:45,034
-Man, if she doesn't love him
after riding in his Maverick,

417
00:19:45,068 --> 00:19:46,862
she's a total ice princess.

418
00:19:46,896 --> 00:19:48,586
-Yep.

419
00:19:53,827 --> 00:19:56,724
-Surprisingly short run-on time
for a '70s car.

420
00:20:00,034 --> 00:20:02,551
[Rooster crows]

421
00:20:02,586 --> 00:20:09,206
-You catch Kotter last night?
-Mother!

422
00:20:09,241 --> 00:20:11,172
-He's the best I could do.
-This is Jody.

423
00:20:11,206 --> 00:20:13,068
-Hello.

424
00:20:13,103 --> 00:20:15,689
-He's just
passing through, Mama.

425
00:20:15,724 --> 00:20:18,344
-Hello, Jody.
-Or should I call you Buffy?

426
00:20:20,551 --> 00:20:23,000
-Supper will be ready directly.

427
00:20:23,034 --> 00:20:24,379
You look like you could do
with something

428
00:20:24,413 --> 00:20:26,551
besides a chili dog
for a change.

429
00:20:26,586 --> 00:20:29,034
-Well, you're right about that.
-Sit right over there.

430
00:20:29,068 --> 00:20:30,655
-Thank you.

431
00:20:30,689 --> 00:20:32,413
-Melissa, go get your father
and tell him,

432
00:20:32,448 --> 00:20:34,931
"We've got company."

433
00:20:34,965 --> 00:20:38,206
-So can you tell me how to get
your daughter in the sack?

434
00:20:38,241 --> 00:20:39,275
-She's a pretty girl,

435
00:20:39,310 --> 00:20:40,931
isn't she?
-Yes, ma'am.

436
00:20:40,965 --> 00:20:42,379
She is.
-Yeah, I can't imagine

437
00:20:42,413 --> 00:20:44,000
where she got it.

438
00:20:44,034 --> 00:20:47,413
-Where'd she find you?
-Down by the pond.

439
00:20:47,448 --> 00:20:50,379
-By the pond?
-Hmm?

440
00:20:50,413 --> 00:20:52,655
-Those walnuts are tearing
through the hay.

441
00:20:55,448 --> 00:20:56,793
-What is it?

442
00:20:56,827 --> 00:21:01,206
-We have company for supper.
-Company, who?

443
00:21:01,241 --> 00:21:05,344
-Nobody you know, just a boy
I met down by the pond.

444
00:21:05,379 --> 00:21:07,344
-A boy you met?

445
00:21:07,379 --> 00:21:09,241
You brought him to the house?

446
00:21:09,275 --> 00:21:12,034
-Yes, I did.
He's nice.

447
00:21:12,068 --> 00:21:13,965
I just want to talk to him.
That's all.

448
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:15,793
-I mean, I might
kill him, but...

449
00:21:15,827 --> 00:21:19,620
-Melissa, you don't
have the right.

450
00:21:19,655 --> 00:21:21,172
-The right?

451
00:21:21,206 --> 00:21:23,862
The right to what,
to talk to someone?

452
00:21:23,896 --> 00:21:26,068
Now don't get so excited.
He's just passing through.

453
00:21:26,103 --> 00:21:27,758
He'll be gone soon.

454
00:21:27,793 --> 00:21:29,620
-Is that all there is to it?

455
00:21:29,655 --> 00:21:33,896
Is it?
You didn't call him,

456
00:21:33,931 --> 00:21:36,068
did you?
-Or mail anything?

457
00:21:36,103 --> 00:21:37,931
-No.

458
00:21:37,965 --> 00:21:43,551
I tell you, it's all right.
-What about Lucinda?

459
00:21:43,586 --> 00:21:45,931
-She can eat in her room.
Now come on up to the house.

460
00:21:45,965 --> 00:21:49,103
-Or you're grounded, Dad.

461
00:21:49,137 --> 00:21:52,724
-Herbert von Karajan
is not pleased with that.

462
00:21:52,758 --> 00:21:54,448
-And be nice to him.

463
00:21:58,655 --> 00:22:01,034
-So, you're just
passing through?

464
00:22:01,068 --> 00:22:03,379
-Yes, ma'am.

465
00:22:03,413 --> 00:22:05,103
-I'm just going to take
some of this stuff with me.

466
00:22:05,137 --> 00:22:11,689
I'm setting up a new apartment.
-All right.

467
00:22:11,724 --> 00:22:14,482
Where's the idiot?
-Daddy, this is Jody Thompson.

468
00:22:14,517 --> 00:22:16,931
-This is my unbelievably
sweaty dad.

469
00:22:16,965 --> 00:22:18,827
-Hello, Jody.
-How's Uncle Bill?

470
00:22:18,862 --> 00:22:20,482
-How do you do?
-You hungry?

471
00:22:20,517 --> 00:22:22,172
I could eat a horse.

472
00:22:22,206 --> 00:22:24,241
-Yeah, I guess I could too.
-Good.

473
00:22:24,275 --> 00:22:26,034
Have a drink, young man.

474
00:22:26,068 --> 00:22:28,448
-Now, Jody, there won't be
any hard feelings

475
00:22:28,482 --> 00:22:29,482
if you don't want any of that.

476
00:22:29,517 --> 00:22:31,586
-Oh, bosh.
-Oh, Hieronymus.

477
00:22:31,620 --> 00:22:34,034
-He'll love this stuff.

478
00:22:34,068 --> 00:22:39,793
Best darn cider in the whole
country, I make it myself.

479
00:22:39,827 --> 00:22:41,793
I, uh, put peanuts in it...

480
00:22:41,827 --> 00:22:43,241
-Ooh, I sure hope
he said peanuts.

481
00:22:43,275 --> 00:22:44,896
-...brings out the taste.

482
00:22:44,931 --> 00:22:46,793
-You know, with Mitchum,
you can skip a week.

483
00:22:46,827 --> 00:22:48,517
Did you know that?

484
00:22:50,793 --> 00:22:57,896
-Well, to BO!
-Man, that's good!

485
00:22:57,931 --> 00:23:01,310
Mm!
-Tastes vaguely of back sweat.

486
00:23:01,344 --> 00:23:04,275
-Supper ready?
-Just about.

487
00:23:04,310 --> 00:23:07,034
Wash up, everybody.

488
00:23:07,068 --> 00:23:17,000
[Doors closing]

489
00:23:17,034 --> 00:23:19,310
♪ Everybody is working
for the weekend ♪

490
00:23:19,344 --> 00:23:20,827
-Whoa.

491
00:23:20,862 --> 00:23:22,586
Holy cow.
Whew.

492
00:23:22,620 --> 00:23:23,896
-What's up, Mike?

493
00:23:23,931 --> 00:23:25,448
-Decided to start
a walnut ranch

494
00:23:25,482 --> 00:23:26,965
like that guy in the film there.

495
00:23:27,000 --> 00:23:28,275
Man, is that hard work.

496
00:23:28,310 --> 00:23:29,517
-Hey, I hear you.

497
00:23:29,551 --> 00:23:32,793
-Whew, man, barely got started
and look at me.

498
00:23:32,827 --> 00:23:35,241
-Hey, you look like hell.
-Oh, yeah.

499
00:23:35,275 --> 00:23:38,000
I know, and I was just leafing
through the catalogs.

500
00:23:38,034 --> 00:23:39,172
How do you account for that?

501
00:23:39,206 --> 00:23:42,034
-Well, it's darn hard work,
my friend.

502
00:23:42,068 --> 00:23:43,517
-Man, you said it.

503
00:23:43,551 --> 00:23:45,586
Oh, I'm not going to make fun
of walnut ranchers anymore.

504
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:46,586
I'll tell you that.
-No.

505
00:23:46,620 --> 00:23:47,758
♪ Doo do doo boom boom

506
00:23:47,793 --> 00:23:49,344
♪ Ba boom boom boom

507
00:23:49,379 --> 00:23:52,758
-Oh, Mike, you been walnut
ranching or something?

508
00:23:52,793 --> 00:23:54,931
-Yeah. Look at me.
I'm just ragged.

509
00:23:54,965 --> 00:23:56,827
-Yeah, well, I could've
told you that, man.

510
00:23:56,862 --> 00:23:59,103
Me, I've been pecan farming.

511
00:23:59,137 --> 00:24:00,758
It's like rolling off a log.

512
00:24:00,793 --> 00:24:03,206
Just made my first million, too.

513
00:24:03,241 --> 00:24:06,068
Haven't even grown
a single pecan.

514
00:24:06,103 --> 00:24:09,724
-We'll be right back.
-Mike, could you back off?

515
00:24:09,758 --> 00:24:11,379
You smell like canned tamales.

516
00:24:11,413 --> 00:24:13,758
-Oh, yeah, I'm sorry.
Ugh!

517
00:24:13,793 --> 00:24:19,310
♪♪

518
00:24:20,379 --> 00:24:22,758
-Tell me, Jody,
you think you'll ever stop...

519
00:24:22,793 --> 00:24:25,000
-It's a bit gamy there.
-...bumming around?

520
00:24:25,034 --> 00:24:26,000
-Sure, I will, Mr. Strickland,

521
00:24:26,034 --> 00:24:28,103
as soon as the excitement
wears off

522
00:24:28,137 --> 00:24:29,931
or I find something
I really want.

523
00:24:29,965 --> 00:24:31,517
-He wants my hair,
doesn't he?

524
00:24:31,551 --> 00:24:34,275
-You know, I gave a lot ofthought to this before I did it.

525
00:24:34,310 --> 00:24:36,241
I just didn't jump up and run.

526
00:24:36,275 --> 00:24:39,034
-Well, sounds like you know
what you're about, son.

527
00:24:39,068 --> 00:24:41,827
-I don't actually.
How'd you pick that up?

528
00:24:41,862 --> 00:24:44,931
-How about another little drink
of my cider?

529
00:24:44,965 --> 00:24:46,068
-No, thank you.

530
00:24:46,103 --> 00:24:47,655
Besides, I've got
to get back on the road.

531
00:24:47,689 --> 00:24:50,068
-No, why don't you wait
until morning?

532
00:24:50,103 --> 00:24:52,068
The highway is going
to be foggy tonight.

533
00:24:52,103 --> 00:24:54,586
-Melissa, if Jody
wants to leave,

534
00:24:54,620 --> 00:24:57,586
we really shouldn't
try to keep him.

535
00:24:57,620 --> 00:25:02,103
-Yes, Melissa, if he has
to get to San Francisco...

536
00:25:02,137 --> 00:25:03,413
-Well, I'm in no hurry.

537
00:25:03,448 --> 00:25:05,896
I just don't want to strain
your hospitality.

538
00:25:05,931 --> 00:25:07,310
-Don't be silly.

539
00:25:07,344 --> 00:25:10,965
It's no strain, and besides,
we've got a spare room.

540
00:25:11,000 --> 00:25:13,034
You can be off first thing
in the morning.

541
00:25:13,068 --> 00:25:16,137
-I just mopped up the blood
from the last guy.

542
00:25:16,172 --> 00:25:17,931
-Well, I'd hate to be
any more trouble.

543
00:25:17,965 --> 00:25:20,482
-Well, you are.
-It's no trouble at all.

544
00:25:20,517 --> 00:25:23,827
-To kill you.
-Is it, Mother?

545
00:25:23,862 --> 00:25:26,034
-No, I'll just tell
Leatherface you're here.

546
00:25:26,068 --> 00:25:28,241
-Of course not.

547
00:25:28,275 --> 00:25:31,241
I'll fix up the sewing room.
-The sawing room!

548
00:25:31,275 --> 00:25:33,448
-I'll go with you in case
you need any help.

549
00:25:33,482 --> 00:25:34,724
-Should we have Jody
as our guest,

550
00:25:34,758 --> 00:25:36,482
or should I just drill
right into my molar?

551
00:25:36,517 --> 00:25:37,965
-I sure appreciate it.

552
00:25:38,000 --> 00:25:39,827
-Don't say anything
more about it.

553
00:25:39,862 --> 00:25:41,379
You and I can pick up
the dishes,

554
00:25:41,413 --> 00:25:42,862
and then we'll go for a walk.

555
00:25:42,896 --> 00:25:44,896
-We pick them up for a second
and set them down.

556
00:25:44,931 --> 00:25:47,275
It's an odd custom.

557
00:25:47,310 --> 00:25:50,862
-Do you like living out here
like this in the country?

558
00:25:50,896 --> 00:25:52,448
-I suppose so.

559
00:25:52,482 --> 00:25:54,344
It's the only way
I've ever lived.

560
00:25:54,379 --> 00:25:56,310
-Didn't you ever want
to do something different?

561
00:25:56,344 --> 00:25:57,620
-Like what?

562
00:25:57,655 --> 00:25:59,655
-Oh, I don't know,
just something different.

563
00:25:59,689 --> 00:26:02,310
-Not everyone can be
like you, you know?

564
00:26:02,344 --> 00:26:03,310
You're lucky.

565
00:26:03,344 --> 00:26:05,344
-Your death will be quick.
-Yeah.

566
00:26:05,379 --> 00:26:06,379
Yeah, I guess I am.

567
00:26:06,413 --> 00:26:07,586
-But you get to watch
the walnuts

568
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:10,862
grow up wild and free.
-What's it like?

569
00:26:10,896 --> 00:26:12,689
-What?
-The steering on your Maverick.

570
00:26:12,724 --> 00:26:16,379
-Being able to do exactly whatyou want to do every single day.

571
00:26:16,413 --> 00:26:19,034
-It's exciting.
-Exciting?

572
00:26:19,068 --> 00:26:20,620
-Exciting?
-Exciting.

573
00:26:20,655 --> 00:26:21,793
-Yes.

574
00:26:21,827 --> 00:26:24,965
Why do you think I turned down
your road today?

575
00:26:25,000 --> 00:26:26,724
-Why did you?

576
00:26:26,758 --> 00:26:28,655
-Because I wanted to see
what was at the other end.

577
00:26:28,689 --> 00:26:30,965
-And your end was
at the other end.

578
00:26:31,000 --> 00:26:33,034
-And I got lucky
because you were there.

579
00:26:35,275 --> 00:26:37,517
-Help, please!
Please!

580
00:26:37,551 --> 00:26:43,241
♪♪

581
00:26:43,275 --> 00:26:46,517
-Huh, girls usually run
a lot farther away from me.

582
00:26:46,551 --> 00:26:52,965
♪♪

583
00:26:53,000 --> 00:26:56,620
-This is where the fish lives.
-Why did you run?

584
00:26:56,655 --> 00:26:59,931
-Because this is where
the fish lives.

585
00:26:59,965 --> 00:27:02,034
-Felt like it.

586
00:27:02,068 --> 00:27:06,896
I really wanted to fly, but I
couldn't do that, so I ran.

587
00:27:06,931 --> 00:27:08,896
-You're kind of an idiot,
aren't you?

588
00:27:13,724 --> 00:27:16,000
-This is where my tongue lives.

589
00:27:20,620 --> 00:27:22,827
-Please, not in front
of the fish.

590
00:27:24,965 --> 00:27:30,103
-Um, he -- hey, your hoof is
on my sneaker.

591
00:27:30,137 --> 00:27:34,413
-Mm, that was really bad.

592
00:27:34,448 --> 00:27:38,344
-Tell me about yourself,
who you are, all of that.

593
00:27:38,379 --> 00:27:41,586
-Where your fish lives.

594
00:27:41,620 --> 00:27:44,068
-Well, my father
is a Russian count,

595
00:27:44,103 --> 00:27:45,379
and when the Bolsheviks came...

596
00:27:45,413 --> 00:27:47,758
-Stop it.
-You don't believe that?

597
00:27:47,793 --> 00:27:50,068
-No.
-Well, it could be true.

598
00:27:50,103 --> 00:27:52,379
-Stop it.

599
00:27:52,413 --> 00:27:54,758
-Oh, sure, Miss This Is
Where The Fish Lives.

600
00:27:54,793 --> 00:27:57,724
-Okay.

601
00:27:57,758 --> 00:27:58,758
My father is a lawyer.

602
00:27:58,793 --> 00:28:00,103
-For the Czars' court.

603
00:28:00,137 --> 00:28:02,000
-And he wants me
to be a lawyer, too.

604
00:28:02,034 --> 00:28:05,310
-Stop it.
-I told him, I didn't know yet,

605
00:28:05,344 --> 00:28:07,448
that I wanted to go out and pokearound the country for a while.

606
00:28:07,482 --> 00:28:08,931
-Stop it.

607
00:28:08,965 --> 00:28:11,931
-So he gave me the car, $300
and a handshake, and here I am.

608
00:28:11,965 --> 00:28:13,034
-Stop it.

609
00:28:13,068 --> 00:28:14,275
-Do you let him know
what you're doing?

610
00:28:14,310 --> 00:28:15,482
-Sure, I do.

611
00:28:15,517 --> 00:28:17,620
I think he'd like to be with me.-Stop it.

612
00:28:17,655 --> 00:28:21,482
-He sounds like a nice man.
-Yes, he's a very nice man.

613
00:28:21,517 --> 00:28:23,137
-Really crooked lawyer though.

614
00:28:23,172 --> 00:28:26,000
Man, he'll embezzle the socks
right off you.

615
00:28:28,551 --> 00:28:32,103
-Hallmark Hall of Fame presents
"The Touch of Satan."

616
00:28:32,137 --> 00:28:36,827
♪ Come sit with me
and Satan too ♪

617
00:28:36,862 --> 00:28:43,793
♪ He's your friend and mine

618
00:28:43,827 --> 00:28:46,551
-You know, I like you.
I did right away.

619
00:28:46,586 --> 00:28:53,448
-Like most women.
-You like me too, don't you?

620
00:28:53,482 --> 00:28:56,034
Don't you?
-Uh-huh.

621
00:28:56,068 --> 00:28:58,758
-In a Ron Palillo
sort of way, sure.

622
00:29:01,413 --> 00:29:03,310
-Funny, isn't it?

623
00:29:03,344 --> 00:29:06,206
I wonder what causes a thing
like that.

624
00:29:06,241 --> 00:29:08,068
-Maybe I hexed you.

625
00:29:08,103 --> 00:29:10,034
-You mean,
put the whammy on me?

626
00:29:10,068 --> 00:29:12,172
-Maybe.
-Nope, not me.

627
00:29:12,206 --> 00:29:16,172
-I already had the whammy
when I was little.

628
00:29:16,206 --> 00:29:19,517
-Will you stay here a few days?
-By this log, and don't move.

629
00:29:19,551 --> 00:29:21,172
-Huh?
-Stay here with me.

630
00:29:21,206 --> 00:29:22,586
-What?
-Stay here.

631
00:29:22,620 --> 00:29:24,413
-Huh?
-Stay here!

632
00:29:24,448 --> 00:29:26,551
-Well...
-I'm lonely, Jody.

633
00:29:26,586 --> 00:29:28,931
I need someone to talk to,

634
00:29:28,965 --> 00:29:32,379
to share things with,
just for a few days.

635
00:29:32,413 --> 00:29:34,482
-Someone to share
clunky dialogue with.

636
00:29:34,517 --> 00:29:37,000
-Well, I was going to be
in San Fran...

637
00:29:37,034 --> 00:29:39,034
-Cisco?
-Well, what about your folks?

638
00:29:39,068 --> 00:29:42,206
-I already live with them.
-Oh, Jody.

639
00:29:42,241 --> 00:29:45,034
-You're such an imbecile.
-You know what I mean,

640
00:29:45,068 --> 00:29:46,896
would they mind?

641
00:29:46,931 --> 00:29:51,551
-No.
-All right.

642
00:29:51,586 --> 00:29:54,034
-I'll leave.
-Mm.

643
00:29:54,068 --> 00:29:58,275
-Oh, do you know how long it's
been since I've felt this good?

644
00:29:58,310 --> 00:29:59,931
-Must be at least 50 years.

645
00:29:59,965 --> 00:30:02,551
-No, since the last guy left,
about a week ago.

646
00:30:06,241 --> 00:30:09,793
-Oh, now he's rubbing his Ryan
O'Neal-y face all over hers.

647
00:30:09,827 --> 00:30:11,551
-Yeesh.
-Ugh.

648
00:30:11,586 --> 00:30:12,758
Ugh.

649
00:30:12,793 --> 00:30:13,827
-Let's get up, first thing
in the morning.

650
00:30:13,862 --> 00:30:15,344
We can have
the whole day together.

651
00:30:15,379 --> 00:30:16,413
-I'll wear my goat's head.

652
00:30:16,448 --> 00:30:18,241
It'll be fun.

653
00:30:18,275 --> 00:30:20,793
-What's so funny?
-You.

654
00:30:20,827 --> 00:30:22,034
You are.
Come on.

655
00:30:22,068 --> 00:30:23,517
Let's go.
-Put on the gloves.

656
00:30:23,551 --> 00:30:25,103
Go six rounds.
See what you're made of.

657
00:30:25,137 --> 00:30:26,862
Come on.
Let's go.

658
00:30:30,103 --> 00:30:33,275
-Night, fish.
-Goodnight, kid, glub, glub.

659
00:30:33,310 --> 00:30:38,689
♪♪

660
00:30:38,724 --> 00:30:40,413
-Uh, just want to drive
through the director

661
00:30:40,448 --> 00:30:42,517
of photography credit
real quick.

662
00:30:45,344 --> 00:30:50,344
-Instant ball and chain,
I tell you.

663
00:30:50,379 --> 00:30:52,758
-Great "Bold Ones" tonight,
huh, honey?

664
00:30:54,724 --> 00:30:57,620
-Did you have a nice walk?
-Yes, ma'am.

665
00:30:57,655 --> 00:31:02,034
-Um, don't come up for about
5 minutes, okay.

666
00:31:02,068 --> 00:31:03,758
-Good night.

667
00:31:05,758 --> 00:31:09,724
-I sure love
the yelling channel.

668
00:31:09,758 --> 00:31:14,103
-Huh, your folks seem
nice and evil.

669
00:31:16,931 --> 00:31:18,275
-That's your room.

670
00:31:18,310 --> 00:31:20,103
-There's ton of varnished bread
laying around in there.

671
00:31:20,137 --> 00:31:24,310
Just push it out of the way.
-Huh, nice door.

672
00:31:24,344 --> 00:31:28,344
I'd like to see it crumpled up
next to my bed... Oh, wait.

673
00:31:28,379 --> 00:31:30,206
-Bang on my door
in the morning?

674
00:31:32,344 --> 00:31:33,758
-At 6:00?

675
00:31:33,793 --> 00:31:36,827
-Sure, why not.
-Okay.

676
00:31:36,862 --> 00:31:38,965
-We'll have to get up
and milk the walnuts.

677
00:31:39,000 --> 00:31:40,689
-Hmm.

678
00:31:44,206 --> 00:31:46,413
-Good night, creepy,
vacuous girl.

679
00:31:51,068 --> 00:31:53,344
-Uh, so I don't need
a kiss or anything.

680
00:31:53,379 --> 00:31:55,000
I'm fine.

681
00:31:55,034 --> 00:31:56,931
[Door closes]

682
00:31:56,965 --> 00:32:01,724
-[Humming]

683
00:32:01,758 --> 00:32:05,724
-I like to sniff my blinds
before I go to bed.

684
00:32:05,758 --> 00:32:08,862
-Hey, Beelzebub,
Kali, and Lucifer!

685
00:32:10,965 --> 00:32:13,620
-Huh.

686
00:32:13,655 --> 00:32:16,517
-Hey, a signed copy
of the Necronomicon here.

687
00:32:16,551 --> 00:32:18,275
-"You guys scare me,
ha, ha, ha.

688
00:32:18,310 --> 00:32:20,000
Signed, Satan."

689
00:32:24,862 --> 00:32:26,827
-Ah! Its a hideous thing
made of skin!

690
00:32:26,862 --> 00:32:28,551
Oh, it's just me.

691
00:32:32,310 --> 00:32:34,034
-Ah, that represents
all my hang-ups, man,

692
00:32:34,068 --> 00:32:36,827
and I just let them fall.

693
00:32:36,862 --> 00:32:39,379
Cool.

694
00:32:39,413 --> 00:32:42,655
-Huh.
-Hmm.

695
00:32:45,620 --> 00:32:47,034
-Uh, excuse me,

696
00:32:47,068 --> 00:32:51,482
I think I left a Sustacal back
in the corner there.

697
00:32:51,517 --> 00:32:54,586
-Go away.
-Who are you?

698
00:32:54,620 --> 00:32:57,137
-My name is
Margaret Rawhide-Chew.

699
00:32:57,172 --> 00:32:58,517
-Go!

700
00:32:58,551 --> 00:33:00,034
-Hmm.

701
00:33:00,068 --> 00:33:03,482
-I usually like the left side
of the bed if that's okay.

702
00:33:06,931 --> 00:33:08,103
-Huh.

703
00:33:08,137 --> 00:33:10,655
Did a plucked turkey
in a wig just talk to me?

704
00:33:19,517 --> 00:33:20,586
-Uh, front desk?

705
00:33:20,620 --> 00:33:22,586
There's a mummy in my room.

706
00:33:25,000 --> 00:33:29,344
-Uh, Mrs. Rotting-Pumpkinhead?
Hello?

707
00:33:29,379 --> 00:33:30,655
-Who is it?

708
00:33:30,689 --> 00:33:34,965
-It's me.
-Is Captain Hook there?

709
00:33:35,000 --> 00:33:37,241
-I've got to talk to you.
-Do you have a needle?

710
00:33:37,275 --> 00:33:39,413
I need to reinflate
your grandma.

711
00:33:41,620 --> 00:33:43,689
-What's the matter?

712
00:33:43,724 --> 00:33:45,896
-Is there anybody else in this
house that I don't know about?

713
00:33:45,931 --> 00:33:47,068
-Well, you know about

714
00:33:47,103 --> 00:33:49,275
Mrs. Wilma Failed-Genetic-
Experiment, right?

715
00:33:49,310 --> 00:33:52,103
-Lucinda.
-She came right in my room

716
00:33:52,137 --> 00:33:54,896
and scared the hell out of me.

717
00:33:54,931 --> 00:33:56,965
-I'm sorry she frightened
you, Jody.

718
00:33:57,000 --> 00:33:58,758
-She only kills
when she's crazy.

719
00:33:58,793 --> 00:34:00,655
-But you mustn't pay
any attention to her.

720
00:34:00,689 --> 00:34:02,655
She's an old woman,
and she likes to wander

721
00:34:02,689 --> 00:34:03,896
around the house.

722
00:34:03,931 --> 00:34:06,413
It won't happen again.

723
00:34:06,448 --> 00:34:09,068
-Who is she?
-She's my great-grandmother.

724
00:34:09,103 --> 00:34:12,862
-She's not that great.
-What happened to her face?

725
00:34:12,896 --> 00:34:15,413
-She was badly burned
when she was a young girl.

726
00:34:15,448 --> 00:34:18,689
That's why she doesn't go out
very much because of her face.

727
00:34:18,724 --> 00:34:20,241
-Unless she's got
a coffee coupon.

728
00:34:20,275 --> 00:34:22,172
-Go back to bed.

729
00:34:22,206 --> 00:34:24,586
She was just curious.

730
00:34:24,620 --> 00:34:26,310
I'll go talk to her.

731
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:32,827
-Okay.

732
00:34:32,862 --> 00:34:34,103
-Oh, and there's a huge,
insane,

733
00:34:34,137 --> 00:34:36,344
scabby gorilla
wandering around too.

734
00:34:36,379 --> 00:34:41,103
-Why did she say go away?
-She's afraid of strangers.

735
00:34:41,137 --> 00:34:42,620
-Well, she wasn't afraid
to come into my room

736
00:34:42,655 --> 00:34:44,000
without knocking.

737
00:34:44,034 --> 00:34:46,034
-She's an old lady, Jody!

738
00:34:46,068 --> 00:34:48,275
-They don't know what they're
doing half the time.

739
00:34:48,310 --> 00:34:49,724
-I tell you what,
I'll go flip her left

740
00:34:49,758 --> 00:34:52,000
turn signal on, and she'll
go right back to sleep.

741
00:34:52,034 --> 00:34:55,965
-I -- I'm sorry.
Go back to bed.

742
00:34:56,000 --> 00:34:58,413
-Uh, could you make
your grandma wear a bell?

743
00:34:58,448 --> 00:35:01,172
-Okay.

744
00:35:01,206 --> 00:35:06,655
♪♪

745
00:35:11,482 --> 00:35:14,448
-I'm going to
lock the door now.

746
00:35:14,482 --> 00:35:16,068
Do you understand?

747
00:35:16,103 --> 00:35:19,241
-I haven't understood anything
since McKinley went down.

748
00:35:19,275 --> 00:35:21,448
-[Mumbling]

749
00:35:21,482 --> 00:35:24,103
-Uh, she's saying never try
to deep-fry a snowball.

750
00:35:24,137 --> 00:35:26,344
-No.
It's late.

751
00:35:26,379 --> 00:35:30,310
-Huh.
-You have to go to sleep now.

752
00:35:30,344 --> 00:35:33,586
Tomorrow I'll take you
for an extra long walk.

753
00:35:33,620 --> 00:35:36,862
-All the way to the dressing
table and back.

754
00:35:36,896 --> 00:35:39,827
-[Mumbling]

755
00:35:39,862 --> 00:35:41,827
-You know, there's a delightful
scene later in the movie

756
00:35:41,862 --> 00:35:43,241
where this old woman raps.

757
00:35:43,275 --> 00:35:46,862
-Wow.
-No, there isn't.

758
00:35:46,896 --> 00:35:48,758
-Good night...
-And by "Good night"

759
00:35:48,793 --> 00:35:53,137
I mean, "Shut up."
-...my sister.

760
00:35:57,241 --> 00:35:59,827
-Oh, I just wanted to give
her a Werther's.

761
00:36:02,689 --> 00:36:03,965
-Oh, man.

762
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:05,413
Now a withered, old Billy Zane
is going to come in

763
00:36:05,448 --> 00:36:08,517
and give her a withered old
coeur de la mer.

764
00:36:10,689 --> 00:36:12,137
-Yeah.
-Ah, good day.

765
00:36:12,172 --> 00:36:14,241
Took a walk.
Got that dress sewn.

766
00:36:14,275 --> 00:36:16,000
Killed.

767
00:36:20,448 --> 00:36:28,034
-Ah, a wrinkle!
-Ooh!

768
00:36:28,068 --> 00:36:31,793
Oh, it's kind of a lateral move,actually.

769
00:36:31,827 --> 00:36:34,137
-She turned into
Cecilia Bartoli.

770
00:36:34,172 --> 00:36:35,379
-Huh.

771
00:36:35,413 --> 00:36:37,793
-I miss my Eddie Munster
widow's peak.

772
00:36:37,827 --> 00:36:45,655
♪♪

773
00:36:45,689 --> 00:36:49,965
-I've still got to work
on the mustache, though.

774
00:36:50,000 --> 00:36:53,482
-Ah, back to being
Swifty Lazar.

775
00:36:53,517 --> 00:36:55,034
-Hmm.
-Who's got it going on?

776
00:36:55,068 --> 00:36:57,275
Me!
That's who!

777
00:36:57,310 --> 00:36:59,586
-What else are we going
to do today, hmm?

778
00:36:59,620 --> 00:37:01,758
-Well, would you mind
driving me to town

779
00:37:01,793 --> 00:37:03,448
so I can go to the store?

780
00:37:03,482 --> 00:37:05,965
-Sure.
-Ow, my pet tick!

781
00:37:06,000 --> 00:37:08,068
-I'll go change and tell them
we're leaving.

782
00:37:08,103 --> 00:37:09,379
Meet me at the car.
-Okay.

783
00:37:09,413 --> 00:37:13,172
-Hey, I got rooked.

784
00:37:13,206 --> 00:37:20,551
-The cup is glued to my lip.
-The mom looks like Ron Popeil.

785
00:37:20,586 --> 00:37:22,344
She does.
-I asked Jody

786
00:37:22,379 --> 00:37:24,206
to stay a few days,
and he agreed.

787
00:37:24,241 --> 00:37:26,965
-He agreed that she asked him?
-Figured you would.

788
00:37:29,896 --> 00:37:32,758
-Jody is going to drive me
to the store.

789
00:37:32,793 --> 00:37:34,931
-Does she know
everything we need?

790
00:37:34,965 --> 00:37:36,172
-Yes.

791
00:37:36,206 --> 00:37:38,310
-We'll probably stop by where
the fish lives.

792
00:37:41,620 --> 00:37:44,620
-I said Jody has agreed
to stay a few days.

793
00:37:44,655 --> 00:37:46,068
-We heard you the first time.

794
00:37:46,103 --> 00:37:48,275
-We weren't interested
then either.

795
00:37:50,379 --> 00:37:53,034
-And it's all right with you?

796
00:37:53,068 --> 00:37:54,517
-No, it's not all right
with me,

797
00:37:54,551 --> 00:37:56,724
but it's your decision,
not mine.

798
00:37:56,758 --> 00:37:59,206
-Uh, Mike, if you're ever
this family's secret Santa,

799
00:37:59,241 --> 00:38:02,448
don't buy them pot holders.

800
00:38:02,482 --> 00:38:04,482
-That means a great deal
to me, Luther.

801
00:38:04,517 --> 00:38:06,275
-I mean, Mr. Vandross.

802
00:38:06,310 --> 00:38:09,172
[Rooster crows]

803
00:38:09,206 --> 00:38:10,379
-Thank you.

804
00:38:10,413 --> 00:38:14,172
♪ Is this the little demon
I carried ♪

805
00:38:14,206 --> 00:38:16,034
-Go to the store.

806
00:38:16,068 --> 00:38:19,172
-Buy canned peaches
and dishwashing liquid.

807
00:38:23,965 --> 00:38:27,827
-I should stop
dying my eyebrows.

808
00:38:27,862 --> 00:38:30,620
-Melissa.
-Hmm?

809
00:38:30,655 --> 00:38:32,482
-What are you going
to tell him?

810
00:38:32,517 --> 00:38:34,862
-I don't know.

811
00:38:34,896 --> 00:38:37,310
I'll have to wait and see.

812
00:38:37,344 --> 00:38:39,068
[Footsteps]

813
00:38:39,103 --> 00:38:41,482
-Are they miking her
shoe bottoms?

814
00:38:41,517 --> 00:38:42,827
-Well, time to stick my cup

815
00:38:42,862 --> 00:38:45,517
back on my lips.

816
00:38:45,551 --> 00:38:47,724
[Rooster crows]

817
00:38:48,655 --> 00:38:51,206
-Oh, yeah.
He's a hot one, all right.

818
00:38:51,241 --> 00:38:53,172
-Oh, and they don't need plant
holders either, Mike.

819
00:38:53,206 --> 00:38:55,482
I mean, you know,
if you're their secret Santa.

820
00:38:55,517 --> 00:38:57,482
-Got it.

821
00:38:57,517 --> 00:39:00,655
-Well, off
to my stewardess job.

822
00:39:00,689 --> 00:39:02,793
-Ready to go?
-Yes.

823
00:39:02,827 --> 00:39:05,275
Let's go.
-Take a left.

824
00:39:05,310 --> 00:39:06,793
Right there.
Good.

825
00:39:06,827 --> 00:39:10,620
♪♪

826
00:39:10,655 --> 00:39:12,793
-Look out!
Walnut stampede!

827
00:39:14,862 --> 00:39:16,793
-Hey, want to play
pitchfork bug?

828
00:39:19,827 --> 00:39:23,241
-Well, now, Andy, I think
there was a demon in the car!

829
00:39:23,275 --> 00:39:24,965
-Ooh.

830
00:39:27,172 --> 00:39:31,482
-Enhance your moody soundtrack
music with tiny fart sounds!

831
00:39:36,000 --> 00:39:40,551
-He's got two huge Sudafed
on top of his car.

832
00:39:40,586 --> 00:39:41,724
See?

833
00:39:41,758 --> 00:39:46,793
Pfft, pfft, pfft, pfft.

834
00:39:51,931 --> 00:39:54,655
-Huh.
From the waist down, a woman.

835
00:39:57,379 --> 00:40:01,379
-Man, I try to keep fit, but
after the third baby, you know.

836
00:40:01,413 --> 00:40:02,793
[Doorbell rings]

837
00:40:02,827 --> 00:40:03,931
-Um, hi.

838
00:40:03,965 --> 00:40:07,655
Have you seen
the other Village People?

839
00:40:07,689 --> 00:40:09,827
-Boy, that body pile is really
growing.

840
00:40:09,862 --> 00:40:11,620
Oh, that Grandma.

841
00:40:14,896 --> 00:40:17,137
-Die, die, die!
I mean, hello.

842
00:40:17,172 --> 00:40:18,586
-Well, hello, Mr. Mason.
-Howdy, Mrs. Strickland.

843
00:40:18,620 --> 00:40:19,827
Luther around?

844
00:40:19,862 --> 00:40:22,137
-I believe he's
around the back.

845
00:40:22,172 --> 00:40:23,724
-You mind if I wander
back there?

846
00:40:23,758 --> 00:40:25,068
-Of course not.
-Thank you, ma'am.

847
00:40:25,103 --> 00:40:28,068
-Just got a real walnut jones
today, ma'am.

848
00:40:28,103 --> 00:40:30,379
-There's not anything wrong,
I hope.

849
00:40:30,413 --> 00:40:31,689
-Oh, no, Mrs. Strickland.

850
00:40:31,724 --> 00:40:33,689
I just want to chew the fat
with Luther for a while.

851
00:40:33,724 --> 00:40:36,413
I'll be seeing you.
-He even brought his own fat.

852
00:40:36,448 --> 00:40:39,241
-Ew.
-Hmm.

853
00:40:39,275 --> 00:40:42,793
Would he even fit
in the iron maiden?

854
00:40:42,827 --> 00:40:45,172
-Well, hello, John.
What brings you out this way?

855
00:40:45,206 --> 00:40:48,448
-And if you say "My squad car,"
I'll chainsaw you in half.

856
00:40:48,482 --> 00:40:49,586
-Oh, nothing much.

857
00:40:49,620 --> 00:40:51,965
Just kind of poking around.
-Sit down.

858
00:40:52,000 --> 00:40:53,931
Take a load off.
-Thank you.

859
00:40:53,965 --> 00:40:56,275
-Got the kettle of hands going,
I see.

860
00:40:58,793 --> 00:41:00,689
-Luther, doesn't the back
of your place butt up

861
00:41:00,724 --> 00:41:02,655
against
old man Larson's property?

862
00:41:02,689 --> 00:41:04,103
-It sure does.

863
00:41:04,137 --> 00:41:06,517
-You don't mind if I chew
on this stick insect, do you?

864
00:41:06,551 --> 00:41:09,310
-You still working on
that Larson business?

865
00:41:09,344 --> 00:41:11,482
-Harris Townley has still got
his wind up.

866
00:41:11,517 --> 00:41:12,620
-Ooh.

867
00:41:12,655 --> 00:41:14,103
-He thinks if we can
break this thing,

868
00:41:14,137 --> 00:41:17,586
he'll be re-elected sheriff for
the next 20 years, damn fool.

869
00:41:17,620 --> 00:41:18,862
He's running around over
in Newport

870
00:41:18,896 --> 00:41:20,482
like he thought
he was J. Edgar Hoover.

871
00:41:20,517 --> 00:41:25,482
-Pretty dress, though.
-How can I help?

872
00:41:25,517 --> 00:41:28,482
-Do you mind if I just take
a look around your place?

873
00:41:28,517 --> 00:41:29,827
-Of course not.

874
00:41:29,862 --> 00:41:32,896
What are you looking for?
-Hell, I don't know.

875
00:41:32,931 --> 00:41:34,448
We ain't found nothing yet.

876
00:41:34,482 --> 00:41:36,724
Not even a suspicious
looking cow chip.

877
00:41:36,758 --> 00:41:37,862
-Hmm.
-Hmm.

878
00:41:37,896 --> 00:41:38,931
-Looks like whoever killed
the old man

879
00:41:38,965 --> 00:41:41,862
went up in a puff of smoke.
-I see.

880
00:41:41,896 --> 00:41:44,689
So you don't have any ideaat all who might have done this.

881
00:41:44,724 --> 00:41:46,103
-Nope.
Nothing.

882
00:41:46,137 --> 00:41:48,413
That's why we're all
just out poking around.

883
00:41:48,448 --> 00:41:51,310
I guess the sheriff hopes
we'll find a signed confession

884
00:41:51,344 --> 00:41:52,689
just laying in the grass.

885
00:41:52,724 --> 00:41:55,482
-Well, help yourself
to my place, John.

886
00:41:55,517 --> 00:41:56,827
Take all the time you want.

887
00:41:56,862 --> 00:42:00,586
-Just don't spook the walnuts.
-Thanks a lot, Luther.

888
00:42:00,620 --> 00:42:03,620
I appreciate that.
-Listen...

889
00:42:03,655 --> 00:42:05,758
-My tummy is growling.
-You get through poking around,

890
00:42:05,793 --> 00:42:09,724
come on up to the house, and
we'll have a touch of my cider.

891
00:42:09,758 --> 00:42:11,379
-I knew you were going to
say that, Luther.

892
00:42:11,413 --> 00:42:13,310
-Then give me the finger
like that.

893
00:42:13,344 --> 00:42:14,724
-But if Sheriff Townley
finds out

894
00:42:14,758 --> 00:42:16,758
I was drinking your cider,

895
00:42:16,793 --> 00:42:19,413
I may have to start
working for a living.

896
00:42:19,448 --> 00:42:23,862
Thanks anyway, Luther.
I'll see you in town.

897
00:42:23,896 --> 00:42:25,275
-Uh, by the way,
there's no reason to look

898
00:42:25,310 --> 00:42:27,000
in the crawl space.
Heh-heh.

899
00:42:30,275 --> 00:42:31,620
-She's going to go buy
a whole bunch

900
00:42:31,655 --> 00:42:35,586
of Procter & Gamble products.

901
00:42:35,620 --> 00:42:40,517
-Why do I suddenly feel hungry
for Carnation Ice Cream?

902
00:42:40,551 --> 00:42:45,137
-I like this place because
they have bulk eye of newt.

903
00:42:45,172 --> 00:42:46,448
-Two, three!

904
00:42:46,482 --> 00:42:51,965
-And then, get this, four!
-Morning, Mr. Gentry.

905
00:42:52,000 --> 00:42:54,103
-Oh, Melissa.
-Please feel free to peruse

906
00:42:54,137 --> 00:42:57,172
my supply of unguents,
suspensions, tinctures,

907
00:42:57,206 --> 00:43:01,482
compounded formulas,
and other sundries.

908
00:43:01,517 --> 00:43:02,827
-Huh.

909
00:43:02,862 --> 00:43:05,482
Anyway, I will shake the paint
off your walls, honey.

910
00:43:08,793 --> 00:43:09,931
-What's the matter
with these people?

911
00:43:09,965 --> 00:43:11,896
You'd think we had
the plague or something.

912
00:43:11,931 --> 00:43:15,034
-It's not you, just me.
-Yep, got the plague.

913
00:43:15,068 --> 00:43:16,206
Want to make out?

914
00:43:16,241 --> 00:43:20,482
-They always behave that way.
-Well, why?

915
00:43:20,517 --> 00:43:24,000
-They say in this town
that I'm a witch.

916
00:43:24,034 --> 00:43:26,896
-A witch?
-It only rhymes with witch.

917
00:43:30,448 --> 00:43:32,896
-Ah, you
plague-ridden little witch!

918
00:43:35,620 --> 00:43:37,724
-Here.
-Ah.

919
00:43:37,758 --> 00:43:38,931
-See what I mean?

920
00:43:38,965 --> 00:43:42,827
-Witches can't lift flour
on their own.

921
00:43:42,862 --> 00:43:45,172
-Why do they think
you're a witch?

922
00:43:45,206 --> 00:43:47,034
-Because I am.

923
00:43:47,068 --> 00:43:48,827
-Oh, stop it.
-You don't believe me?

924
00:43:48,862 --> 00:43:52,931
Ask them.
-She gives them 15.55

925
00:43:52,965 --> 00:43:55,827
just so she can get
6.66 back in change.

926
00:43:59,241 --> 00:44:01,896
-This is a tense moment because
she used to date the grocer.

927
00:44:01,931 --> 00:44:03,068
-Ah.

928
00:44:03,103 --> 00:44:06,206
-Thank you, Melissa.
-You're welcome.

929
00:44:06,241 --> 00:44:10,931
-Then you'll just deliver
the broomsticks to my house?

930
00:44:10,965 --> 00:44:14,517
-Hey, they got a sale
on mismatched beer!

931
00:44:14,551 --> 00:44:17,482
-Hmm.
-Steinlager, Pete's.

932
00:44:19,551 --> 00:44:24,551
-Zah!
-Ah, you made me go burpy.

933
00:44:24,586 --> 00:44:27,000
-Heh, heh, I said "zah."
-Young whipper-snapper.

934
00:44:27,034 --> 00:44:28,310
-Where's he from?

935
00:44:28,344 --> 00:44:30,137
-The grocer's wife died
when someone said "zah."

936
00:44:30,172 --> 00:44:31,310
It's not funny.

937
00:44:31,344 --> 00:44:33,344
-Why did you do that?
-Serves them right.

938
00:44:33,379 --> 00:44:37,172
-You just made it worse doing
a silly thing like that.

939
00:44:37,206 --> 00:44:40,172
-I'm sorry, Melissa,
but that sort of thing bugs me.

940
00:44:40,206 --> 00:44:42,620
-Come on.
Let's go.

941
00:44:42,655 --> 00:44:45,689
-I've said "zah" in better
towns than this.

942
00:44:45,724 --> 00:44:47,275
-After the movie,
remember to pick up

943
00:44:47,310 --> 00:44:50,896
some Carnation Ice Cream!

944
00:44:50,931 --> 00:44:56,379
♪♪

945
00:44:57,241 --> 00:45:00,344
-Okay.
Here we go.

946
00:45:00,379 --> 00:45:01,344
Anything?

947
00:45:01,379 --> 00:45:04,000
-Hmm.
Hmm.

948
00:45:04,034 --> 00:45:05,827
Nope.
Nope, nope, I'm fine, Mike.

949
00:45:05,862 --> 00:45:08,310
I guess all we can do
is add more rocks.

950
00:45:08,344 --> 00:45:11,344
-Okay.
-You know, I'm worried.

951
00:45:11,379 --> 00:45:14,586
If I wasn't a witch, I'd be deadby now or at least shrieking

952
00:45:14,620 --> 00:45:17,758
out a confessions or raining
down curses on my tormentors,

953
00:45:17,793 --> 00:45:19,655
but instead, hey, I'm great.

954
00:45:19,689 --> 00:45:21,862
I couldn't be better.

955
00:45:21,896 --> 00:45:22,965
-Okay.

956
00:45:23,000 --> 00:45:24,758
Anything?
-Oh, no.

957
00:45:24,793 --> 00:45:26,689
Did you actually add one?

958
00:45:26,724 --> 00:45:28,793
No, I'm fine.
Very comfortable, in fact.

959
00:45:28,827 --> 00:45:31,068
I can't believe this!
-Yeah, Crow, what I don't get

960
00:45:31,103 --> 00:45:33,413
is why do you assume you're
a witch in the first place.

961
00:45:33,448 --> 00:45:34,931
-Well, I --
I don't really assume it, Mike,

962
00:45:34,965 --> 00:45:36,965
but I guess it's always been in
the back of my mind, you know?

963
00:45:37,000 --> 00:45:38,758
-Hmm, yeah, but you know, Crow,

964
00:45:38,793 --> 00:45:40,448
the reason you can survive
all of this

965
00:45:40,482 --> 00:45:43,517
is because of
your durable molybdenum frame.

966
00:45:43,551 --> 00:45:45,413
-Well, that's nice of you
to say, Mike,

967
00:45:45,448 --> 00:45:46,793
but let's face it,
I'm a witch!

968
00:45:46,827 --> 00:45:48,862
You might as well burn me
at the stake.

969
00:45:48,896 --> 00:45:50,965
-Crow, you're not a witch,

970
00:45:51,000 --> 00:45:54,068
but, you know, it is hard for meto prove a negative, you know?

971
00:45:54,103 --> 00:45:57,862
I can't really proveyou're not a frog, for instance.

972
00:45:57,896 --> 00:46:00,103
-Uh! Mike, this is weird.
-Oh, what?

973
00:46:00,137 --> 00:46:01,241
Are --are you feeling
some pain?

974
00:46:01,275 --> 00:46:02,379
Is it working?
-No, how did you know

975
00:46:02,413 --> 00:46:03,896
I was thinking
I might be a frog?

976
00:46:03,931 --> 00:46:05,137
Did I tell you that?

977
